  <h2>Why do published digital human prices disagree?</h2>

  <p>Because one word covers several products. The Chinese term is digital human, shuziren, and the cards rarely say which product they price.</p>

  <p>Start at the cheap end.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Self-serve digital human tools in China price a likeness clone of a real person in the hundreds to low thousands of RMB: one seller's own card lists 398 RMB a likeness and 9.9 RMB a voice, and single-use cards relayed in November 2025 list 1,680 RMB for a likeness and 75 to 599 RMB for a voice. The same tools ask for about 20 minutes of single-person footage to build the clone.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published Chinese digital human rate cards, one undated and one relayed November 2025, collected 10 September 2026, prices as each card states them, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>That is a self-serve product. The buyer uploads footage and gets an avatar that talks to a script.</p>

  <p>A bespoke build sits on a different table, with a schedule attached.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A Chinese price table published in April 2026 puts a template presenter at 0 to 5,000 RMB over one to three days, a custom 2D presenter captured from a real person at 8,000 to 30,000 RMB over five to ten days, a 2.5D semi-photoreal presenter at 30,000 to 80,000 RMB, and a photoreal 3D digital human at 80,000 to 300,000 RMB and up over 20 to 45 days.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a Chinese sourcing intermediary's published price table, April 2026, collected 10 September 2026, quoted prices with no sample or method.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same April 2026 table prices capabilities on top of the build: real-time interaction adds 10,000 to 50,000 RMB, and each additional language 3,000 to 10,000 RMB.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a Chinese sourcing intermediary's published price table, April 2026, collected 10 September 2026, quoted prices with no sample or method.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">High-end managed digital human services start around 49,999 RMB on a card relayed in November 2025.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published Chinese digital human rate cards relayed in a seller's comparison, November 2025, collected 10 September 2026, no vendor named.</p>

  <p>The top of the range is where the cards stop agreeing.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published 2026 Chinese prices for a photoreal 3D character do not agree. One July 2026 review lists 20,000 to 100,000 RMB a character and 100,000 to 1,000,000 RMB for film-grade motion capture; an April 2026 table lists 80,000 to 300,000 RMB and up for the character alone.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: two Chinese price tables, April and July 2026, one from a sourcing intermediary and one from a site affiliated with a seller, collected 10 September 2026, neither stating a method.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Four years earlier the numbers were larger. Practitioners interviewed by a Chinese product-management publication in August 2022 put an enterprise anime-style virtual human at about 100,000 RMB on average, a high-fidelity one at 300,000 to 500,000 RMB, and called 2 to 3 million RMB a reasonable ceiling for a photoreal one.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Chinese industry media feature, August 2022, figures quoted from founders and directors of companies selling these builds, no sample or method.</p>

  <p>Neither 2026 page explains its gap with the other. The 2022 quotes priced whole projects, rigging and motion capture and operation included, before four years of tooling changes. A midpoint of any of these would be a price nobody quoted, so this page prints each range as published.</p>

  <p>Then look at who publishes these numbers at all.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Across three Chinese buyer searches for digital human prices, 12 of 18 unique results were published by a seller of digital humans or a site affiliated with one, none was published by a government body, research institute or trade association, and none stated a method for its prices.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: search-results audit run 10 September 2026, publisher type recorded for every result, no domain named.</p>

  <p>That is why every price here is labeled a market claim. It is also why the quote in your inbox matters more than any figure on this page.</p>

  <h2>Replica, custom character or stock avatar: which do you need?</h2>

  <p>Decide the product before comparing prices. The three options differ in build cost. They differ more in whose consent sits under the asset, and in what goes wrong once it is live.</p>

