  <p>That table is production practice, not a ranking, and the consent column is not legal advice. The same national rules on synthesis, labeling and livestreaming attach to all three rows. Nothing in any instrument read for this page exempts a stock face.</p>

  <h2>What does it cost to run a virtual livestreamer?</h2>

  <p>Nobody publishes that number in a form that can be checked. Recorded video is a different matter, because it is priced like software.</p>

  <p>A July 2026 Chinese review of digital human software lists pay-per-minute generation at 0.5 to 7 RMB a minute, individual monthly tiers at 28 to 300 RMB, enterprise annual plans from 2,980 RMB, one-time software licenses at 980 to 9,980 RMB, and private on-premises deployment from 120,000 RMB. The site that published it is affiliated with a seller and discloses no testing method.</p>

  <p>Volume packages follow the same logic.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">A May 2026 Chinese package card prices digital human video by volume: 999 RMB a month for up to 50 videos, 2,999 RMB for up to 200, and 7,999 RMB for an unlimited tier the publisher puts at about 4 RMB a video.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a Chinese seller's published package card, May 2026, collected 10 September 2026, per-video figures are the seller's own division.</p>

  <p>The per-video figure is the seller dividing its own price. It says nothing about how many of those videos a brand would approve. The <a href="/resources/insights/all-in-cost-of-ai-video">article on the all-in cost of AI video</a> walks through the lines a generation price leaves out, and the <a href="/solutions/ai-production/video">AI video production page</a> describes the recorded-video side of this work.</p>

  <p>Livestreaming is where the published evidence runs out.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The only published figure found for a livestreaming digital human is a reference quotation, not a measured project: 65,000 RMB to build, 30,000 RMB of server hardware, and 12,000 RMB a year of maintenance.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: a Chinese sourcing intermediary's published price table, April 2026, collected 10 September 2026, labeled by its publisher as a reference case.</p>

  <p>No Chinese-language page read for this article publishes a per-hour, per-month or per-account price for a digital human livestream license that could be read at source. None publishes the staffing cost of running one with a stated method. That comes from a review of Chinese rate cards on 10 September 2026, covering three Chinese buyer queries and eleven price pages read in full. One page on livestream pricing returned an error, and the search was cut short, so treat the absence as provisional.</p>

  <p>Staffing figures do circulate, as anecdotes without a method. None appears here.</p>

  <p>So a livestream budget has lines this page cannot price: the license, the people running the room, and the hours on air. Get each quoted in writing, in the unit you will be billed in.</p>

  <h2>What consent does a digital human built from a real person need?</h2>

  <p>Consent comes in layers, and each layer comes from a different instrument. What follows is production practice built on the published text, not legal advice.</p>

  <p>The base layer is China's Civil Code. Under Articles 1019, 1021 and 1022, in force since 1 January 2021, a likeness may not be made, used or published without the portrayed person's consent. A disputed license clause is construed in favor of the person portrayed. Where the license term is unagreed or unclear, either party may end it on reasonable notice. That statute text was read through Chinese legal commentary, not on an official host.</p>

  <p>The last of those rules is the replica's quiet risk. A digital human on a license with no clear term rests on a permission either side can end.</p>

  <p>Livestreaming adds a consent rule of its own, set by Article 25 of the Measures for the Administration of Online Live Streaming Marketing (Trial), and it reaches voice.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Article 25 says that where a livestream room operator or livestream marketer uses another person's likeness as a virtual image for livestream marketing, it must obtain the portrait right holder's consent and must not infringe that right by forging it through information technology, and that a natural person's voice is protected the same way.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Online Live Streaming Marketing (Trial), Article 25, April 2021; full text as reproduced on a provincial government portal, read 10 September 2026, matched by two secondary readings.</p>

  <p>The capture session itself can produce personal data of the most protected kind.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Personal Information Protection Law lists biometric identification among the categories of sensitive personal information, and requires the individual's separate consent to process sensitive personal information.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Personal Information Protection Law of the People's Republic of China, Articles 28 and 29, in force 1 November 2021, statute text read on the Cyberspace Administration of China's site, 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>The law lists biometric identification without defining it. This page does not say every face scan qualifies. It says the question gets asked before capture, not after.</p>

  <p>The editing tools are addressed too, under the Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis of Internet Information Services.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Article 14 of those Provisions says that where a deep synthesis service provider or technical supporter offers a function that edits biometric information such as a face or a voice, it must prompt the user to inform the individual being edited in accordance with law and to obtain that individual's separate consent.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis of Internet Information Services, Article 14, instrument text retrieved 9 September 2026.</p>

  <p>Note whom that article addresses: the provider or technical supporter, which prompts the user. It does not say which of a brand, an agency or a vendor collects the consent. That is generally treated as a matter for the contract.</p>

