  <h2>Is a digital human an advertising endorser in China?</h2>

  <p>No instrument read for this page answers that. The question is open.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">China's Advertising Law defines an advertising endorser as a natural person, legal person or other organization, other than the advertiser, that recommends or certifies goods or services in its own name or image, and says an endorser may not recommend or certify goods it has not used or services it has not received.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Advertising Law of the People's Republic of China, Articles 2 and 38, as amended 29 April 2021. Statutory text.</p>

  <p>Whether a digital human, or the person it was cloned from, fits that definition is not settled by anything read here.</p>

  <p>The Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, in force since 1 May 2023, address livestream roles directly.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same Measures put advertiser duties on a seller who promotes by livestream, and agency, publisher or endorser duties on the room operator and the on-camera talent depending on their role.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Internet Advertising, Article 19, in force 1 May 2023. Statutory text.</p>

  <p>No article of those advertising measures mentions a virtual or AI presenter. A brand putting product claims in a digital human's mouth is working in that gap, and should know it.</p>

  <h2>Which rules apply to a digital human in China?</h2>

  <p>National instruments name digital persons and AI-synthesized virtual hosts in so many words. No national rule read for this page is written only for digital human livestreams. The working set is below, with CAC standing for the Cyberspace Administration of China.</p>

