  <p>Start with the definition, because it is the one place a national instrument names the product.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis of Internet Information Services define deep synthesis technology to include three-dimensional reconstruction, digital simulation and similar technologies that generate or edit digital persons and virtual scenes.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis of Internet Information Services, Article 23, instrument text read 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis of Internet Information Services were issued jointly by the Cyberspace Administration of China, the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology and the Ministry of Public Security, released 11 December 2022 and in force since 10 January 2023.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, December 2022, regulator's release of the instrument.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Article 17 requires a conspicuous label in a reasonable position or area of the generated or edited content where a deep synthesis service may cause public confusion or misrecognition, and it lists synthetic voice and voice imitation, and face generation, face replacement, face manipulation and posture manipulation, among the services that trigger it. Article 18 forbids any organization or individual from using technical means to delete, alter or conceal those labels.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, Provisions on the Administration of Deep Synthesis of Internet Information Services, Articles 17 and 18, instrument text retrieved 9 September 2026.</p>

  <p>On the decision table, Article 17 bears most directly on the replica row. It reaches a custom character wherever that character may cause confusion.</p>

  <h2>Does a digital human need an AI label in China?</h2>

  <p>The labeling measures cover every kind of content a digital human produces. They never name the product.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Measures for Labeling of AI-Generated Synthetic Content were issued jointly by the Cyberspace Administration of China, the Ministry of Industry and Information Technology, the Ministry of Public Security and the National Radio and Television Administration, released 14 March 2025 and in force since 1 September 2025.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, March 2025.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Measures for Labeling of AI-Generated Synthetic Content cover text, images, audio, video, virtual scenes and other information generated or synthesized with artificial intelligence. Video carries its visible label on the opening frame and around playback, audio a spoken or rhythm cue, and a virtual scene a label on the opening frame. The instrument never uses the words digital human, virtual human or livestream.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, March 2025, instrument text read in full on 10 September 2026, Articles 3 and 4.</p>

  <p>Two more articles follow the file after it leaves the tool.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Article 4 of the Measures says that when a service provider offers download, copy or export functions for generated synthetic content, it must ensure the file contains a conforming explicit label.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, March 2025, instrument text.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Article 10 of the Measures says a user publishing generated synthetic content through a distribution service must proactively declare it and use the labeling function the provider supplies, and that no organization or individual may maliciously delete, alter, forge or conceal a label, or supply tools or services for others to do so.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, March 2025, instrument text.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The mandatory national standard GB 45438-2025, Cybersecurity technology, Labeling method for content generated by artificial intelligence, was published 28 February 2025 and took effect 1 September 2025, the same day as the Measures.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: national standards register, State Administration for Market Regulation, February 2025.</p>

  <p>Those articles address service providers and users. Between a brand, an agency and a vendor, who applies the label is generally treated as a matter for the contract, so write it into one. The <a href="/resources/insights/china-ai-labeling-rules-production-workflow">China AI labeling rules article</a> follows the label through a production pipeline, including where it tends to fall off.</p>

  <h2>What do China's livestream rules say about virtual hosts?</h2>

  <p>They anticipate them, and they put the first obligation on the platform.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Measures for the Administration of Online Live Streaming Marketing (Trial) were issued by the Cyberspace Administration of China, the Ministry of Public Security, the Ministry of Commerce, the Ministry of Culture and Tourism, the State Taxation Administration, the State Administration for Market Regulation and the National Radio and Television Administration, and took effect on 25 May 2021.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, release of the Measures, April 2021.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Article 13 of those Measures says a livestream marketing platform must carry out a security assessment under the relevant rules, and label the content conspicuously, where a virtual image displayed through artificial intelligence, digital vision, virtual reality, speech synthesis or similar technology is used for livestream marketing.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Online Live Streaming Marketing (Trial), Article 13, April 2021; full text as reproduced on two government legal portals, read 10 September 2026, the two texts identical.</p>

  <p>Article 13 names the platform. It refers to the relevant rules without naming them, so this page cannot say what the security assessment consists of.</p>

  <p>What the host says on air is covered too.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same Measures bar livestream marketers from publishing false or misleading information that deceives or misleads users, and from faking transaction, follower, view or like data.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Measures for the Administration of Online Live Streaming Marketing (Trial), Article 18, April 2021; full text as reproduced on two government legal portals, read 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Code of Conduct for Online Hosts, issued by the National Radio and Television Administration and the Ministry of Culture and Tourism in a notice dated 8 June 2022, says in its first article that virtual hosts synthesized with artificial intelligence technology, and their content, follow the code by reference.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: National Radio and Television Administration, notice publishing the Code of Conduct for Online Hosts, document No. 36 of 2022, published 22 June 2022, instrument text read 10 September 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Among the conduct the code prohibits are exaggerated promotion that misleads consumers, false promises, absolute terms, and selling counterfeit or infringing goods while faking traffic data.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: National Radio and Television Administration and Ministry of Culture and Tourism, Code of Conduct for Online Hosts, Article 14, June 2022, instrument text read 10 September 2026.</p>

  <p>Read together, a virtual host follows the same code a human host does. That includes the lines on false promises and absolute terms.</p>

  <p>Platform rules are a separate layer, and this page cannot read them. Douyin's safety and trust center and its ecommerce learning center served no rule text to an unauthenticated fetch on 10 September 2026. Nothing here says what Douyin's current terms for a digital human host are. Check them inside the account before a stream is booked. The <a href="/solutions/platforms/douyin">Douyin platform page</a> covers producing for the platform more broadly.</p>

  <h2>What are Chinese regulators acting on?</h2>

  <p>Impersonation, above all. Two 2026 notices from the Cyberspace Administration of China name it.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The Cyberspace Administration of China's April 2026 campaign against AI application abuses lists among its first-phase targets AI face-swap and voice-imitation services offered without the person's authorization, and stealing another person's likeness to livestream or create content through digital virtual human technology.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, campaign notice, 30 April 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same regulator's February 2026 action on unlabeled AI content describes AI face swapping and voice cloning used to impersonate athletes, actors, hosts, entrepreneurs and other public figures.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, 12 February 2026.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Cyberspace authorities reported in February 2026 that they had handled 13,421 accounts and cleared more than 543,000 items of unlawful or non-compliant information over AI-generated content published without an AI label. The release is a campaign report and does not state the period covered or a sample.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Cyberspace Administration of China, 12 February 2026.</p>

  <p>Those totals cover unlabeled AI content in general, not digital humans alone. The April notice is the sharper signal. A replica built from someone's likeness without their authorization is a named target, which puts the consent chain ahead of the build quote in any plan.</p>

  <p>Reviewed September 2026. This page describes production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h2>Questions buyers ask about digital humans in China</h2>

  <h3>How much does a digital human cost in China?</h3>

  <p>Published Chinese rate cards list a self-serve likeness clone at 398 RMB on one undated card, a custom 2D capture at 8,000 to 30,000 RMB, and a photoreal 3D character at 20,000 to 100,000 RMB or 80,000 to 300,000 RMB and up, depending on the 2026 table. Those are different products. No card states a method, so treat each figure as a dated market claim.</p>

  <h3>What does it cost to run a virtual livestreamer?</h3>

  <p>No Chinese-language page read for this article published a per-hour, per-month or per-account license price for a livestreaming digital human, or a staffing cost with a method. The only published figure found is an April 2026 reference quotation: 65,000 RMB to build, 30,000 RMB of server hardware and 12,000 RMB a year of maintenance. Get the license, staffing and hours quoted separately.</p>

  <h3>Do digital humans in China need an AI label?</h3>

  <p>The Measures for Labeling of AI-Generated Synthetic Content cover video, audio and virtual scenes, though the text never says digital human or livestream. The livestream marketing measures separately tell platforms to label virtual images used for livestream marketing conspicuously. Between brand, agency and vendor, who applies the label is generally treated as a matter for the contract. This is production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>Can a digital human host a Douyin livestream?</h3>

  <p>National rules anticipate virtual hosts. The livestream marketing measures address virtual images used for livestream marketing, with a platform security assessment and conspicuous labeling, and the Code of Conduct for Online Hosts covers AI-synthesized virtual hosts by reference. Douyin's own rule pages served no readable rule text to an unauthenticated fetch on 10 September 2026, so confirm the platform's current terms inside your account first.</p>

  <h3>Do I need consent to build a digital human from a real person?</h3>

  <p>Yes, at several layers. The Civil Code bars using a likeness without consent. The livestream marketing measures require the portrait right holder's consent when another person's likeness becomes a virtual image, and protect voice the same way. Biometric identification is sensitive personal information needing separate consent, and providers of face and voice editing must prompt users to obtain it. This is production practice, not legal advice.</p>

  <h3>Is a custom digital human cheaper than a celebrity?</h3>

  <p>No published celebrity replica license fee with a method was found, so no number answers this. The structure does. A celebrity replica is still a licensed real-person replica. The build is one line; the likeness license, the consent paperwork and the risk that a license with no clear term ends on reasonable notice are the others. A character authored from no identifiable person has no portrait holder to pay.</p>
